":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source states planners directed the spacecraft, the subject of the Voyager 2 document, to Uranus.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-003\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"a journey that would take about 4,5 years\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives the same journey duration in the same format.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-004\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source states this directly.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-005\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Saturn\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source names Saturn as the future encounter, not Neptune.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-006\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"had only 6.4 days of close study during its flyby\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives 6.4 days of close study, not 5.5 hours.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-007\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 1's\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source text names Voyager 1 as the first human-made object to fly past Uranus, a different name from the claim's Voyager 2.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-008\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"short-range observations of the planet began Jan. 31, 1987\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives short-range observations beginning Jan. 31, 1987, not long-range observations beginning Nov. 4, 1985.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-009\",\"verdict\":\"PARTIAL\",\"evidence\":\"signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The signal time at the start of observations is stated, but the source calls those observations short-range, not long-range.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-010\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"Light conditions were five-hundred times less than terrestrial conditions\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives five-hundred times less, not 400 times.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-011\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1968\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives the year 1968 and the time zone ED, not 1986 and UT.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-012\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles)\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_a.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source attaches the units the other way round, giving 50,640 kilometers and 81,500 miles.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-013\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"Voyager 2 discovered 11 new moons\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives 11 new moons, not 10.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-014\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives different names for most of the moons, such as Pucka, Portila and Bianca II.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-015\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"obvious allusions to Goethe\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source says the names allude to Goethe, not Shakespeare.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-016\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source dates the tradition to 1687, not 1787, and ties it to Goethe.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-017\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"three new rings in addition to the “older” eight rings\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives three new rings, not two.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-018\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"three new rings in addition to the “older” eight rings\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives eight previously known rings, not nine.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-019\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives a tilt of 66 degrees, not 59.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-020\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source states the magnetic field is off-center.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-021\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"as high as 450 km/h (72400 meters per hour)\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives 450 km/h and 72400 meters per hour, not 450 miles per hour and 724 kilometers per hour.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-022\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers) below the top cloud surface\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives a lake at 479 miles (900 kilometers), not an ocean at 497 miles (800 kilometers).\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-023\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source states the rings were extremely variable in thickness.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-024\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source states the rings were extremely variable in transparency.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-025\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source names Ariele, Umbrella and Titan, not Ariel, Umbriel and Titania.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-026\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"smaller moons\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source describes the five as smaller moons, not larger, and gives different names for three of them.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-027\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers)\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives the same range in the same format.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-028\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly century-long travels\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source says nearly century-long travels, not decade-long.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-029\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source states this about the images of Miranda.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-030\",\"verdict\":\"SUPPORTED\",\"evidence\":\"Uranus itself appeared generally featureless\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source states this directly.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-031\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source says the news was interrupted the same day, not the same week.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-032\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"the tragic Challenger accident that killed six astronauts\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source gives six astronauts killed, not seven.\"},{\"claim_id\":\"M-033\",\"verdict\":\"CONTRADICTED\",\"evidence\":\"during their space shuttle launch Feb. 28, 1986\",\"evidence_source\":\"source_b.md\",\"rationale\":\"The source dates the launch Feb. 28, 1986, not Jan. 28, 1986.\"}]}","ttft_ms":21410,"type":"result","duration_ms":47555,"uuid":"115ee408-9d1d-4257-b4d7-87226b5503fe","ttft_stream_ms":2061,"time_to_request_ms":116,"first_content_frame_ms":2062,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
